>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>A science museum and science centre career in India means working as a curator, exhibit
    developer, education officer, or planetarium educator, almost always inside a government-run
    network, not a large private industry.</strong> Most of India's science museums and science
    centres are run by the National Council of Science Museums (NCSM) under the Ministry of Culture,
    with a smaller number of state-run and private science-outreach organisations filling the rest.
    This is a genuine, meaningful field, but it is also a small, slow-hiring, government-pay-scale one,
    and the honest picture matters more than the appealing job titles. Whichever track you pick, the
    skill portfolio you build inside it, not the umbrella title "science centre career," is what
    unlocks higher income opportunities and earlier financial freedom from here.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Almost all major science museums and science centres in India (Kolkata, Bengaluru, Mumbai, Delhi, and about 20 more) run under one government body: NCSM.</li>
      <li>Real roles inside these centres are curator, exhibition/exhibit-development officer, education officer/science communicator, technical assistant, and planetarium educator, each with its own pay level and entry qualification.</li>
      <li>This is a small field with few openings a year, a multi-stage government recruitment process, and fixed pay-band salaries, not a high-volume or high-paying career path.</li>
      <li>Independent science-communication content and consulting is the one track here with real income upside, and it is worth building deliberately, not treating as a consolation prize.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
